Let $z=a+bi$ be the complex number with $\vert z \vert = 5$ and $b > 0$ such that the distance between $(1+2i)z^3$ and $z^5$ is maximized. We want to maximize $|(1+2i)z^3 - z^5|^2.$ Since $|z| = 5$, we have
\[(1+2i)^2z^6-(1+2i)z^8 - z^{10} +z^6 = f(z).\]
Observe that $f(z)$ is the sum of three terms that all depend on $|z^6|.$ By the triangle inequality, we see that the optimal $z$ in which the distance is largest must be on a radius of $5$ centered at the origin. We observe that $f(z) = g(r)r^6,$ where $r = |z|$ and $g$ does not depend on $r,$ because $f$ has twice the degree of $r$ while the others all at most have degree $3$ in $r.$ To get the optimal $z$, we maximize $g(r)$ over $r.$
Let's assume that $z^6$ is defined to be a positive real number. Expanding the other terms into exponentials so that only contributes using $r$ to the 2nd or 6th power, we obtain that 
\begin{align*}
g(r) =  11r^6 + - 86r^4 + -22r^2 + -40 &= 0 \text{ or }r^6 = -\left(\frac{86}{11}r^6 + \frac{40}{r^6}\right)
\end{align*} 
\end{think}
